      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the relation between high arterial oxygen tension levels (PaO2) and long-term mortality in patients with spontaneous intracerebral hemorrhage treated in the ICU."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "National observational multicenter cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Twenty-one ICUs in Finland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "A total of 3,033 adult patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "None."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Patients were divided into high (> 150\u2009mm Hg), intermediate (97.5-150\u2009mm Hg), and low (< 97.5\u2009mm Hg) PaO2 groups based on the lowest measured PaO2/FIO2 ratio during the first 24 hours after ICU admission: 63% (n = 1,923) were in the low group, 29% (n = 892) were in the intermediate group, and 7% (n = 218) were in the high group; 80% were mechanically ventilated. The primary outcome was 6-month mortality, which occurred in 49% of patients and was significantly more frequent in the high PaO2 group than in the intermediate and low PaO2 groups (61% vs 52% and 46%, respectively, p < 0.001). In univariate analysis, patients in the high PaO2 group had a significantly increased risk of 6-month mortality compared with the low PaO2 group (odds ratio, 1.82; 95% CIs, 1.36-2.42; p < 0.001), but this statistically significant relation was lost after adjusting for markers of severity of illness in a logistic mixed-effects regression model (odds ratio, 1.10; 95% CI, 0.76-1.60; p = 0.598)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "No significant relation between PaO2 levels and long-term mortality was found. The clinical role of hyperoxemia in patients with intracerebral hemorrhage treated in the ICU remains controversial and warrants further studies."
        }
      ]},
